{"site_id": "0d9bd81d-d41c-4381-8e2e-7626928fedc1", "site_name": "Apolyanka", "table": "unified_sites", "column": "lat", "current_value": "48.735780056239456, 30.40534947823009", "proposed_value": null, "reason": "coordinates-unresolved", "note": "The stored point is the OpenStreetMap node of the modern village Apolianka (48.7361, 30.4043); the Trypillia site lies on the plain beside the village. The one readable scholarly page that treats Apolianka itself, the Frontiers in Digital Humanities megasite study, locates it only as 'the CI site of Apolianka (7 km West of Nebelivka)', a direction and distance, not a point, and no readable page prints the settlement's own coordinates, so nothing can be quoted for the site's point.", "phase3": false, "finding_test_id": "WD3/lat", "evidence": []}
{"site_id": "0d9bd81d-d41c-4381-8e2e-7626928fedc1", "site_name": "Apolyanka", "table": "unified_sites", "column": "source_url", "current_value": null, "proposed_value": null, "reason": "field-unresolved", "note": "There is no page about this site itself. The site's own links lead to a Wikipedia article on Cucuteni-Trypillia settlements as a class, to a Kiel CRC 1266 project page, and to the Frontiers megasite study; each treats Apolianka only as one example inside a multi-site report, and the village's own Ukrainian, Russian and Wikidata entries describe the modern village, not the Trypillia settlement. A multi-site report is not a page about this very site, so I leave the field to the owner.", "phase3": false, "finding_test_id": "WD3/source_url", "evidence": []}
